Absolutely. For a first app, it helps to stop thinking of **“upload AAB → publish → wait for Play Console”** as the launch process.

A production mobile app normally has a **release + observability pipeline** surrounding the store. Play Console is only one part of it.

For PayslipMax, I would build a fairly lightweight version of what professional mobile teams use rather than buying an expensive enterprise observability stack immediately.

## 1. What is the industry standard?

A typical professional setup looks roughly like this:

```text
DEVELOPMENT
    │
    ├── Unit tests
    ├── Parser tests
    ├── UI / integration tests
    └── Static analysis / lint
    │
    ▼
CI BUILD
    │
    ├── Build release AAB
    ├── Run automated tests
    ├── Security/dependency checks
    └── Verify release configuration
    │
    ▼
INTERNAL TESTING
    │
    ├── Developer devices
    └── Small trusted group
    │
    ▼
CLOSED / BETA TESTING
    │
    ├── Real users
    ├── Different devices
    ├── Different Android versions
    └── Real-world documents/data
    │
    ▼
OBSERVABILITY
    │
    ├── Crashlytics / Sentry
    ├── ANR monitoring
    ├── Non-fatal errors
    ├── Breadcrumbs
    ├── Performance monitoring
    └── Play Android Vitals
    │
    ▼
STAGED PRODUCTION
    │
    ├── 5%
    ├── 10%
    ├── 25%
    ├── 50%
    └── 100%
    │
    ▼
CONTINUOUS MONITORING
```

The important concept is **observability**.

You don't merely want:

> “The app crashed.”

You want:

> “Version 1.0.3 build 23 crashed on Samsung S24/Android 16 while parsing a 2-page payslip; format detector selected EXTENDED_GRID; exception occurred in GrammarAwareParser line 184.”

That is the professional standard you should aim toward.

